+# Findings
+
+## Finding 1 — CRITICAL — `deposit()` always reverts against the real USDT contract
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94`
+
+```solidity
+usdt.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);   // line 93 — fine
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);                    // line 94 — reverts
+```
+
+Line 93 correctly uses `SafeERC20`. Line 94 does not — it calls `approve` straight
+through the `IERC20` interface, so solidity emits a call that ABI-decodes a `bool` from
+the return data and reverts if fewer than 32 bytes come back. The deployed Tether
+contract's `approve` declares no return value and returns zero bytes. Every deposit
+reverts, for every user, forever. The vault would be immediately and permanently
+unusable.
+
+`MockUSDT` is a stock OpenZeppelin `ERC20`, which returns `true`, so the suite cannot
+see this.
+
+### Evidence — against the real token, on a mainnet fork
+
+Return data length of the real `approve`, measured directly:
+
