+
+Shrunk to a two-call counterexample: one deposit, one withdrawal. Invariant fuzzing is
+seeded randomly, so your counterexample sequence and the exact numbers in the failure
+message will differ from mine — what reproduces is the failure itself, always shrinking
+to "deposit, then withdraw". The second invariant —
+`totalShares` equals the sum of all share balances — holds over 128,000 calls, which
+shows the harness itself is sound and the failure is in the contract, not the driver.
+
+An invariant suite of five lines would have caught this before it reached review. The
+39 unit tests could not, because every one of them asserts a number the implementation
+produces rather than a property the system must hold.
+
+### Fix (described, not applied)
+
+If the fee is meant to accrue to remaining shareholders, subtract only what actually
+leaves:
+
+```solidity
+totalAssetsStored -= netAssets;
+```
+
+With Finding 2's balance-based `totalAssets()` this becomes automatic: the fee stays in
+the aUSDT position, `totalAssets()` reads the position, share price rises, remaining
+holders are paid. If instead the intent is for the fee to go to the protocol, mint the
+corresponding shares to a treasury address — but then update the README, which currently
+promises the opposite.
+
+Either way, add the invariant `aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this)) >= totalAssets()` with
+equality up to accepted dust, and run it in CI.
